---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const lensCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: '10*x/(x - 10)', label: 'image distance (f = 10 cm)', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.2 }],
  x: { min: 12, max: 60, label: 'object distance do (cm)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 60, label: 'image distance di (cm)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/optics-lenses-and-mirrors">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Light bends when it changes speed — at every glass surface. A curved lens or mirror uses that
    bending to reunite rays, and one thin-lens equation organises all of it:
    <strong>1/f = 1/do + 1/di</strong>, where f is the focal length, do the object distance and
    di the image distance. Everything about cameras, eyes, microscopes and telescopes follows.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the lens equation as a curve — as the object approaches the focal point, the image runs away to infinity" widget="plotter" config={lensCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve like a photographer. Object far away → image lands just past f (the sensor
    plane). Object at 2f → image at 2f, same size (the "1:1" macro case). Object slides toward
    f = 10 cm → image distance explodes — that's the projector regime, throwing a giant image
    forward. Inside f the maths flips sign: the image is virtual, upright, magnified — a
    magnifying glass.
  </p>

  <h2>The rules of thumb</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Refraction:</strong> light entering a denser medium bends toward the normal (glass slows it to ~2/3 of c). The bending angle is Snell's law — the reason a straw looks broken in water.</li>
    <li><strong>Converging vs diverging:</strong> convex lenses and concave mirrors focus; the opposite shapes spread rays whose extensions meet behind — always virtual, upright, smaller.</li>
    <li><strong>Your eye:</strong> a ~17 mm focal-length lens whose <em>cornea</em> does most of the bending; the lens fine-focuses by squashing (accommodation). Glasses just add a correcting lens in series.</li>
    <li><strong>Telescopes gather, not magnify, first:</strong> light-gathering area is what reveals faint galaxies — which is why aperture is the spec that matters (see the astronomy ladder).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Snell (1621); OpenStax, College Physics (Geometric Optics).</p>
</TopicPage>
